      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(channels): honor configured read target policies",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nChannel message actions did not consistently apply the active account and conversation target\npolicies before provider reads or mutations. The affected paths included message, reaction, pin,\nmember, search, and information actions across bundled channel plugins.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThis moves target authorization into each channel owner's action boundary and carries trusted\ncurrent-conversation context through Gateway, agent, plugin, Codex, and Copilot tool paths.\nProvider content is fetched only after the action has resolved and accepted its target.\n\nThe implementation also aligns adjacent provider contracts:\n\n- Feishu authorizes message-id reaction paths before provider content reads and preserves\n  reaction ownership during clear-all.\n- Microsoft Teams uses the shared guarded-fetch transport rather than incompatible custom\n  `globalThis.fetch` wrappers.\n- Google Chat advertises only its supported outbound text action and rejects outbound attachment\n  aliases before provider access while preserving inbound attachments.\n- External channel plugins retain valid exact-current target aliases while cross-conversation,\n  wrong-account, and target-kind-confusion cases continue to fail closed.\n\n## User Impact\n\nCurrent-conversation actions, valid provider aliases for the current conversation, and explicitly\nconfigured cross-conversation actions continue to work. Provider-accessible targets outside the\nconfigured policy fail closed before content reads or mutations. Google Chat outbound\nservice-account behavior is text-only; inbound text, threads, and attachments remain supported.\n\n## Evidence\n\nExact candidate: `2d6897f024a013933c9cd5f4add7a132667d3113`\n\n- Fresh Codex autoreview: no actionable findings.\n- Exact-tree changed checks and production build passed.\n- Focused shared authorization and provider tests: 193 passed.\n- Exact-content QA Channel, bus, runner, and shared authorization tests: 103 passed.\n- Live exact-head provider proof passed for Discord, Slack, Feishu/Lark, Google Chat, Matrix,\n  Microsoft Teams, iMessage, Telegram, and Nextcloud Talk.\n- iMessage passed all required exact-current aliases and ID-only action forms against the approved\n  conversation with provider-side verification.\n- Telegram passed a real inbound sticker, production cache population, model-selected targetless\n  sticker search, and visible reply.\n- Nextcloud Talk passed all five equivalent current-room forms with provider-side reaction\n  verification; unsupported removal produced no provider mutation.\n- Stable `2026.6.11` upgraded in place to the exact candidate core, Codex, and Nextcloud Talk\n  packages. The same persisted session executed delegated provider actions before upgrade, after\n  upgrade, and after candidate restart.\n- Active release matrix: 89 passed, 0 product failures, 0 external blockers, and 4 not applicable\n  across 93 scenario rows; all 4 validation rows passed.\n",
        "branch": "fix/channel-read-policy-gates",
